  produced a failure, that failure would have been retained verbatim with its full stack trace.

  Two false positives pre-empted for reviewers of this archive:
    1. A reviewer grepping for retry will find a spring retry application library in the reactor.
       It is a root version property consumed by three child modules and has nothing to do with
       test reruns. No rerun count is configured in any of the 145 POMs and no retry plugin is
       declared anywhere, so a baseline failure is real and reproducible, and fail fast is safe.
    2. Coverage exclusions are not test exclusions. The coverage plugin in the root aggregator
       excludes eight package globs from coverage measurement only. No test is disabled by them,
       and none of them matches the person plugin package. Relatedly, its agent preparation
       execution declares an output file but no property name, so it publishes the agent argument
       through the default argument line property export; the unit test runner declaration this
       migration adds must therefore not set a literal argument line element, or instrumentation
       is severed silently and the coverage check evaluates an empty data set as a false pass.

  Integration tests are absent from this folder by design, not by omission. This module has seven
  integration test classes, and the integration test runner's two goals carry no phase override,
  so both bind after the test phase and a unit test run produces no report for any of them. None
  is fabricated here. One of those classes carries a pre existing ignored method, which is
  consequently not representable in this archive and may never be cited as a baseline failure. An
  absent integration test is neither a pass nor a failure. Across the tree the correct arithmetic
  is 21 representable, plus 5 sitting on integration test classes, 26 in total.

  Audited for JVM arguments that would open or export an otherwise encapsulated platform package
  to the unnamed module: none appear in this file, and none appear in the production launch
  configuration this migration ships. That zero is earned rather than assumed, because every
  accessibility failure encountered during the migration was inside a test library and was
  resolved by upgrading or removing that library, every reflective access widening call site in
  the codebase targets the application's own classes, and there is no security manager usage
  anywhere. The register of documented platform access exceptions is referred to here by title
  only.

  Namespace invariance: the platform's removal of the enterprise XML binding, annotation and
  activation modules is compensated by adding javax namespace artifacts once in the root
  aggregator and inheriting them reactor wide, never by rewriting source. Every javax.* import in
  this module stays javax.*. This module is the reactor's densest consumer of the removed XML
  binding API, so this archive is close range evidence for that remedy.
